course: reflection
lesson: 19
title: Picked on: freeze-outs, robberies and standing up
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Being picked on day after day makes the dread start the night before: Ethan, whose bag gets pushed off the bench every day by a group of Year 6 boys who use a word about who someone might love as an insult, lies in bed thinking: "Why me? What's wrong with me? Tomorrow it's gonna happen again."
explain: Every morning his tummy went tight; for a month he faked being ill on Mondays; in the end he told Mum, she spoke to the school, the boys were stopped, and even then he kept his head down in that corridor for a while, because fear takes longer to leave than the bullies do.
explain: What he came to understand: nothing is wrong with him; using who someone might be as an insult is bullying; bullies pick anyone who looks like they won't fight back; and the fear of tomorrow is often worse than tomorrow itself. "It's not banter if only one side is laughing."
explain: Not all bullying is loud: freezing someone out on purpose is bullying too, and a mugging is a crime, not a test of courage; handing over trainers to stay safe is smart, because things can be replaced and you can't.
explain: Standing up does not have to mean fighting: sitting with the friend, going to a teacher together, or reporting it is what an upstander does.
fact: Using who someone is, or who they might love, as an insult is bullying; nothing is wrong with the person it is aimed at.
fact: "It's not banter if only one side is laughing": a joke that only the joker enjoys is picking on someone.
fact: The fear of what will happen tomorrow is often worse than the day itself, and the fear can stay for a while even after the bullying stops.
fact: Freezing someone out means ignoring them on purpose, as a group; it is a kind of bullying, and it is about the group's power, not something wrong with the person.
fact: Being mugged means being robbed in the street; handing over things to stay safe is smart, not weak, and it should be told to a parent and the police.
fact: When someone hurts us, we often hurt back harder; "tit for tat" makes a quarrel escalate, and saying sorry for your own part helps even if they started it.
fact: Not wanting to fight is not being soft; walking away is often the braver choice, whatever some grown-ups say.
fact: A bystander only watches; an upstander helps, even in small ways like sitting with the person or telling a teacher together.
fact: "Ducked" means pushed under water; if someone does it to you at a pool, tell a lifeguard or grown-up.
fact: Being called "teacher's pet", "keeno" or "swot" for trying is common teasing; trying your best is good, and other keen kids are out there.
fact: Brothers and sisters fight, but hitting hard is not okay; big feelings like "I wish he didn't exist" pass, and parents need to set rules.
fact: Liking things others call babyish is fine; "you do you" is slang for be yourself.
exercise: Ethan dreads every school day. Why do the boys pick him? | Bullies pick anyone who looks like they won't fight back | Something really is wrong with Ethan and they spotted it | They choose whoever is best at sport in the class | Ethan must have done something terrible to them first
exercise: What does "it's not banter if only one side is laughing" mean? | A joke that hurts only one person is really picking on them | Banter is only allowed when one person is laughing | All jokes are fine as long as at least somebody is laughing at them | Nobody should ever laugh at anything in school
exercise: After the boys were stopped, Ethan still kept his head down in that corridor. Why? | Fear can take longer to leave than the bullying itself | The boys were secretly still bullying him every day | He had forgotten the bullying and just liked the floor | His mum told him to look at the floor in corridors
exercise: What did NOT help Ethan? | Changing his route to hide | Telling his mum what was going on | A buddy walking with him in the corridor | The school's anti-bullying plan
exercise: Jade's friends move away whenever she sits down, and won't say why. What is this? | A freeze-out, a kind of bullying | A normal game that all friend groups play | Proof that Jade did something terrible | A sign that they are planning a surprise
exercise: What helped Jade in the end? | A teacher helping her find a new group, and Mum listening | Begging the old group every day to say what was wrong | Eating lunch alone in the library for the rest of the year | Freezing out a different girl so she would feel better
exercise: A boy punched Lucas and took his trainers. Lucas thinks he's a wimp. What is true? | Handing them over was smart; things can be replaced | He should have fought back, however big the boy was | It was not serious, so there is no need to tell anyone | He should go and take the trainers back by force tomorrow
exercise: Maddie's friend laughed at her drawing, and Maddie said something much meaner back. What does this show? | When we're hurt we often hit back harder, and it escalates | Mean words only count if you were the one who said something mean first | The friend deserved it, so Maddie did nothing wrong | Saying something meaner always ends the argument
exercise: Archie's dad says, "Next time, hit him first." Archie doesn't want to. What is true? | Not wanting to fight isn't soft; walking away can be brave | His dad is right that hitting first always works best and ends fights | He must fight to prove he loves his dad | Wanting peace means he will never stand up for himself
exercise: Isla is scared to help her bullied best friend. What small thing can she do? | Go with her friend to tell a teacher | Fight all the bullies at once in the playground | Pretend she doesn't know her friend any more | Wait until the bullies get bored on their own
exercise: What is an upstander? | Someone who helps when another person is picked on | Someone who stands up whenever a teacher walks in | Someone who joins in with the bullies to stay safe | Someone who stands at the side and just watches
exercise: A bigger kid keeps pushing Noah under in the pool. What helped? | Telling the lifeguard with Dad | Giving up swimming for good so it can't happen | Pushing the bigger kid under water back | Swimming only at night when nobody is there
exercise: Mia gets called "keeno" every time she answers. Should she act dumb? | No; trying your best is good, and she can find keen friends | Yes; it is always better to hide how clever you are | Yes, but only until she leaves primary school and nobody minds any more | No; she should call the others dumb out loud instead
exercise: Kai's brother gave him a black eye over the TV. Kai thinks, "I wish he didn't exist." What is true? | The feeling will pass, but parents need to set rules on hitting | It means Kai truly hates his brother and will hate him forever now | Kai should hit back harder to make it fair | Siblings are allowed to hit as hard as they want
exercise: Lily's friends tease her for liking a kids' cartoon. What helped her? | Her mum saying she still watches cartoons too | Throwing away every babyish thing she owned so nobody could see it | Pretending to like only grown-up films | Teasing her friends about their hobbies
exercise: What does "you do you" mean? | Be yourself | Do whatever the others are doing | Do your homework on your own | Stop talking about yourself
test: Ethan's mate says, "they're just bantering, man, take a joke." ("Banter" means friendly teasing.) What's the best answer? | It's not banter if only one side is laughing | He's right, all teasing is friendly banter | Ethan should laugh along to make it a joke | It only counts if a teacher hears it
test: What would happen if Ethan kept faking Monday tummy aches and told nobody? | The bullying would likely go on and the dread would grow | The boys would get bored and stop by themselves within a week or so | His tummy aches would become the real problem | The school would find out without being told
test: Jade's mum from Bristol says, "They'm being proper mean, my lover, it ain't you." ("My lover" is a West Country way of saying dear.) What is she telling Jade? | The freeze-out is about the group, not something wrong with Jade | Jade should love the group more so they come back | Jade is the one being mean really, and she should go and say sorry to them | It's a normal game and Jade should play along
test: Lucas's cousin goes, "you let him take your creps?? that's so mid." ("Creps" are trainers; "mid" means rubbish.) What's the true reply? | Staying safe was the smart choice; trainers can be replaced | He's right; you should always fight for your shoes | He's right; it was weak and Lucas should be ashamed | It was fine because the trainers weren't new
test: In a film, a kid gets mugged, chases the robber and wins the fight. What's the real-life rule? | Stay safe, hand things over, tell a parent and the police | Always chase the robber and fight to get things back | Never tell anyone, because police don't care about kids | Carry something to fight back with next time
test: Maddie thinks: "She started it, so I don't have to say sorry." Which is wiser? | Say sorry for her own part, even if the friend started it | Wait for the friend to say sorry first, however long | Say something even meaner back so that the two of them are even | Never speak to that friend again after this
test: Archie's grandad says, "In my day we settled it with fists, lad." Archie still doesn't want to. What's true? | Walking away is often braver, and fighting can hurt both | Grandad is right; fists are the only way to settle it | Archie is soft and should toughen up by fighting | He should fight just once, so that his grandad will be proud of him
test: Isla's friend from Wales says, "Come here, give us a cwtch, we'll go to Miss together." ("Cwtch" means a cuddle.) What makes this a good plan? | Going to a teacher together helps and is safer than alone | Cuddling the bullies will make them stop | It is only good if they fight the bullies after | Teachers only listen when two kids come at once
test: Noah's thinking, "nobody cares, I'm never going swimming again, ever." What actually changed things? | Going back with Dad and telling the lifeguard | Staying home every swimming day for a year | Learning how to push bigger kids under the water before they push him | Swimming in a different pool alone
test: Mia's classmate goes, "ugh, she's such a pick-me, always got her hand up." ("Pick-me" is slang for someone trying too hard to be noticed.) What's the true view? | Trying in class is good, whatever the name-calling says | She should stop putting her hand up so they like her | Being keen at school is something to be ashamed of | Only teacher's pets ever answer questions, so she should stop doing it
test: Lily's big brother says, "cartoons are for babies, it's giving toddler." ("It's giving" means it seems like.) What's true? | Liking cartoons is fine; people like different things | He's right; cartoons are only for very small kids | She should hide it carefully so that nobody at school ever finds out | Grown-ups never ever watch any cartoons
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the deliberate exclusion of a pupil by her peer group? | It constitutes a form of bullying attributable to the group's conduct | It is an ordinary social custom carrying no harmful consequence | It invariably indicates a grave fault on the part of the excluded pupil | It is permissible provided that no words are exchanged
exam: Identify the principal reason for which surrendering property during a street robbery is regarded as prudent. | Personal safety takes precedence, as property may be replaced | Surrender obliges the assailant to return the property | Resistance is invariably successful in recovering the property | Property surrendered is automatically insured by the school
exam: It is given that a pupil, upon being mocked, retaliated with a more severe insult. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Such retaliation tends to escalate the dispute, and an apology for one's own part assists | The original mockery wholly excuses any subsequent insult, however severe | The more severe insult resolves the dispute forthwith | Apologies are required solely of the party who commenced the dispute
exam: In respect of the term "upstander", determine which description is correct. | A person who acts to assist another who is being mistreated | A person who observes mistreatment without intervening | A person who participates in mistreatment to avoid it | A person who rises from his seat upon a teacher's entry
